/*! \page developer_template_explicit_page Implicit vs. Explicit Templates

\ref developer_template_defs_page  (Prev)  &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;
\ref developer_param_page          (Next)
<BR>

The PSCF source code contains two different types of class templates, 
which we refer to here as "implicit" and "explicit" templates. They differ 
in how files are organized, and in how they are treated by the compiler
and build system:

<ul>
   <li>
   <b> Implicit class templates </b> : These are C++ class templates 
   for which the source code is organized so as to allow the compiler
   to perform implicit instantiation of the template as needed when 
   processing other files. This is the usual recommended way of writing 
   C++ class templates, as described in most textbooks. </li>

   <li>
   <b> Explicit class templates </b>: These are class templates that 
   are designed to be use by explicitly instantiating all required 
   specializations, and then later linking to the resulting object code. 
   </li>

</ul>
The use of an "explicit" class template is generally feasible only for 
class templates for which there exists only a small, foreseeable set of 
possible choices of template parameters. It is only in this case that 
is may be practical to explicitly instantiate all required specializations. 

For a standard "implicit" template, a preprocessor directive that 
includes the header file for that template must include definitions 
for all of its member functions. This can be accomplished either by 
placing all such definitions in the class header file, or by having 
the header file include a template implementation (.tpp) file that 
contains some or all of these function definitions. 

For an "explicit" class template, however, the template header file 
generally does not contain or include definitions of all member 
functions. In this case, definitions of non-inline member functions 
are instead placed in a separate file that is not included by the 
header file, much like the division of class definition and member
function definitions into separate files that is usually used for
non-templated classes.

In the PSCF code base, implicit class templates are used throughout 
the Util namespace. Explicit templates are instead common in the Pscf 
namespace in code that is used to model periodic systems.
The simplest use of explicit class templates in PSCF is for classes in
the Pscf for which the the dimension of space D is the only template 
parameter. Specializations of such class template with D=1, 2 and 3 
are compiled by explicit instantiation and later linked to create 
executables.  

The heavy use of explicit template instantiation in PSCF avoids some 
of the most common disadvantages of templates in C++. By avoiding the 
need to include member function definitions into other files that 
include the header for a class template, and guaranteeing that each 
specialization is only compiled once, use of explicit instantiation 
reduces compilation times.  By breaking the build process into 
smaller steps, it tends to simplify interpretation and debugging of 
syntax error that occur during development. The main cost is the 
need for explicit instantiation definitions and definitions, 
which introduces a few lines of boilerplate code per class.

\section developer_template_explicit_imp_sec Implicit class templates

Implicit class templates are designed allow the safe use of implicit
instantiation.  All of the container class templates that are defined
in the src/util/containers directory, such as DArray and FSArray, are
implicit templates.

The class definition for each implicit class template is contained in 
a header file that ends with file suffix .h. A preprocessor directive 
to include this header file must also automatically include definitions 
of all class member functions. This requirement can be satisfied by 
organizing the code for an implicit class template in either of two ways:
<ol>

   <li> Definitions of all class member functions may be placed in the
   class template header file. </li>

   <li> Definitions of non-inline class member functions may be placed 
   in a separate template implementation file with file name suffix 
   .tpp, but this file must then be included by the header file. </li>

</ol>
Case 1 is the most common way of organizing files for a class template,
as recommended in most C++ textbooks and other instructional material.  

In case 2, the header file must contain a C/C++ preprocessor "#include" 
directive that includes the .tpp file. The include statement must appear
after the class template definition, and is usually is placed at or near 
the end of the header file. The effect is to create a translation unit 
as seen by the compiler that is equivalent to the header file used in
case 1.

\section developer_template_explicit_exp_sec Explicit class templates

The source code for the PSCF programs for periodic functions contains many
class templates for which there exist only a few valid choices of values
for the template parameters, and thus only a few possible valid template
specializations. Most such classes are written as "explicit" class 
templates, which are designed to be compiled by explicit instantiation.

The code required to use an explicit class template generally involves 
the following components:

  - A class template definition, which includes declarations for all
    member functions. 

  - Explicit instantiations declarations for all required specializations

  - Definitions of inlined class template member functions

  - Definitions of non-inlined class template member functions

  - Explicit instantiation definitions for all required specializations

In PSCF, code for most explicit class templates is organized as follows:

  - A \*.h header file contains the class template definition, any 
    inline member functions, and explicit specialization declarations 
    for all required template specializations.

  - A \*.tpp template implementation file includes the \*.h header 
    file, and contains definitions of all non-inline member functions.

  - One or more compilable \*.cpp and/or \*.cu source files include 
    the \*.tpp implementation file (and thus indirectly include the 
    header file), and contain explicit specialization definitions 
    for all required template specializations.

Placing explicit specialization declarations for all required 
specializations in the header file guarantees that implicit instantiation
will be suppressed for all such specializations in any file that includes 
the header file. Most of the class templates in the Pscf::Rp namespace 
have code that is organized as described above.

\section developer_template_explicit_example1_sec Example 1: Pscf::Basis<D>

Class templates for which the dimension of space, D, is the only template 
parameter are always written using the simple pattern described above. 
In this case, the PSCF build system always explicitly instantiates three 
specializations with the three allowed template argument values of
 D=1, 2, and 3.  As an example of this, we consider the three source 
code files associated with the class template Pscf::Basis. 

The header file src/pscf/crystal/Basis.h contains a definition of the 
class template Pscf::Basis, definitions of a few inline functions, and 
explicit specialization declarations.  The key contents of this file, in 
skeleton form, look like this:
\code
#ifndef PSCF_BASIS_H
#define PSCF_BASIS_H

namespace Pscf {

   // Class definition
   template <int D>
   class Basis {

      // Member declarations

   };

   // Inline member function definitions
   ...

   // Explicit instantiation declarations
   extern template class Basis<1>;
   extern template class Basis<2>;
   extern template class Basis<3>;

}
#endif
\endcode
Note that the header file contains explicit instantiation declarations 
for all valid specializations.

An associated template implementation file, src/pscf/crystal/Basis.tpp, 
contains definitions of all non-inline member functions for the Basis 
class template. A skeleton version of this file looks something like this:
\code

// Include directive for header file
#include "Basis.h"

namespace Pscf {

   // Non-inline member function definitions
   ....

}
\endcode
Note that the implementation file for an explicit class template must 
include the header file, as shown in the above example. Usage for an 
implicit class template template would instead require the header file 
to include the implementation file.

The compilable source file src/pscf/crystal/Basis.cpp includes the 
implementation file Basis.tpp and contains explicit instantiation 
definitions for D=1, D=2, and D=3:
\code

// Include directive for template implementation file
#include "Basis.tpp"

namespace Pscf {

   // Explicit template definitions
   template class Basis<1>;
   template class Basis<2>;
   template class Basis<3>;

}
\endcode
The PSCF build system compiles the file src/pscf/crystal/Basis.cpp and
places the resulting object code for the three specializations Basis<1>, 
Basis<2> and Basis<3> in an object file named Basis.o. The contents of 
this object file are then incorporated into the static library file 
src/pscf/libpscf.a. 

Separation of the code for this sort of class template into three files, 
as in the above example, is common in PSCF but is not the only possible 
organization. If explicit instantiation definitions for all required 
specializations are given in a single \*.cpp file, as in the above 
example, then definitions of non-line functions could instead be placed 
directly in the \*.cpp file, thus making the \*.tpp file unecessary.
The next example, however, discusses a case in which the use of a
separate \*.tpp file is a more important part of the design.

\section developer_template_explicit_example2_sec Example 2: Pscf::Rp::System<D,T>

The Pscf::Rp namespace defines a large set of class templates that are 
used to construct two different executable files for variants of the 
pscf_rp program that provide the equivalent features but use different 
computational "backends". The pscf_rpc executable uses a C++ backend 
that relies on standard CPU hardware, whereas the pscf_rpg executable 
uses a CUDA backend that uses a GPU for mathematical operations on 
large arrays.

Almost all of class template in the Pscf::Rp namespace accept two template
parameters:

   - An int parameter D that represents the dimension of space

   - A class parameter T whose argument must be a "backend identifier
     class"

A "backend identifier class" is a class that is designed specifically to 
be used as a template argument that identifies a choice of computational 
backend.  PSCF currently defines two backend identifier classes named 
Pscf::CPT and Pscf::CUT. Use of class T=Pscf::CPT as template argument 
specifies the use of the C++ backend in the resulting template 
specialization. Use of T=Pscf::CUT as an argument instead indicates the 
use of the CUDA backend. 

We focus here  on how the use of a combination of standard C++ and CUDA 
C++ in order to support the use different backends affects the organization
of source code files for class templates. Files that contain standard C++ 
and CUDA C++ must be compiled using different compilers. The pattern rules 
used by the PSCF makefile system require that files that end with the file 
name suffix \*.cpp must be standard C++ files that can be compiled by a 
standard C++ compiler (e.g., the gcc or clang compiler). Files with a file 
name suffix \*.cu are assumed to be CUDA C++ files that must be compiled 
by the NVIDIA nvcc compiler.  Any specialization of the type of class 
template discussed here that uses T=Pscf::CUT as a template argument 
may contain CUDA code, and thus must be compiled by the NVIDIA nvcc 
compiler. Any specialization of such a template that uses a template 
argument T=Pscf::CPT can only contain standard C++ code, and so should
be compiled using a conventional C++ compiler.  These conventions require
that, for each class template that takes a template identifier class as 
a template parameter, we must create both a \*.cpp file that contains 
all required explicit instantiation definitions for specializations 
with T=Pscf::CPT and a \*.cu file that contains explicit instantiation 
definitions for specializations with T=Pscf::CPT.

As an simple example of this, we consider the source code files associated
with the class template Pscf::Rp::System. This template is declared in the 
file src/rp/system/System.h as:
\code
namespace Pscf {
namespace Rp {
   template <int D, class T> class System;
}
}
\endcode
where template parameter T represents a backend identifier class.
There are four files associated with this class template, all of which 
are located in the directory src/rp/system:

   - A header file named System.h that contains the class template 
     definition, definitions of inline member functions, and explicit 
     instantiation declarations for all allowed specializations of the 
     template.

   - A template implementation file named %System.tpp that includes the
     header file and contains definitions for all non-inline member
     functions.

   - A compilable C++ file named System.cpp contains explicit 
     instantiation definitions for three specializations with D=1,2,3 
     and T=Pscf::CPT.

   - A compilable CUDA C++ file named System.cu that contains explicit
     instantiation definitions for specializations with D=1,2,3 and 
     T=Pscf::CUT.

The System.cpp and %System.cu files both include the System.tpp file, 
and thus also both indirectly include the System.h header file.

The contents of the System.cpp file thus look something like this:
\code

#include "System.tpp"
#include  <pscf/backend/cpp/CPT.h>

namespace Pscf {
namespace Rp {

   // Explicit instantiation definitions
   template <1, CPT> class System;
   template <2, CPT> class System;
   template <3, CPT> class System;

}
}
\endcode
The System.cu file is similar to this, except for the replacement of 
CPT by CUT in each of the explicit instantiation definitions, giving:
\code

#include "System.tpp"
#include  <pscf/backend/cuda/CUT.h>

namespace Pscf {
namespace Rp {

   // Explicit instantiation definitions
   template <1, CUT> class System;
   template <2, CUT> class System;
   template <3, CUT> class System;

}
}
\endcode
The use of a separate template implementation file is essential to this
design, because it allows the same implementation file to be included 
by both the \*.cpp and \*.cu files, without also including non-inline 
function definitions in the header file.

The system used by PSCF to support the use of multiple backends is 
discussed in much greater detail \ref developer_backend_page "here".

